---
import Post from "../../../layouts/Post.astro";
import SleepNav from "../../../components/SleepNav.astro";
import record from "../../../data/sleep/record.json";
const frontmatter = { title: "Sleep targets", description: "Provisional sleep-performance milestones, evidence-supported directions and the criteria for claiming improvement.", updated: "2026-10-22" };
const latest = record.entries.at(-1);
const ouraEntries = record.entries.filter((entry) => entry.oura?.score != null && entry.oura.score_eligible !== false);
const corosEntries = record.entries.filter((entry) => entry.coros?.sleep_score != null);
const latestOura = ouraEntries.at(-1);
const latestCoros = corosEntries.at(-1);
---
<Post frontmatter={frontmatter}>
  <SleepNav current="targets" />
  <p><strong>Objective:</strong> achieve consistently excellent sleep, then expand the measurements and improve its demonstrated contribution to alertness, cognitive performance and training recovery.</p>
  <p>This is version 1 of the target specification, clarified through 22 October. The numerical score milestones below are <strong>proposed project benchmarks</strong>. They are not clinical cutoffs, adopted treatment targets or evidence that a particular lifespan has been gained. The {record.entries.length} dated entries include {ouraEntries.length} eligible Oura whole-night sleep scores and {corosEntries.length} available COROS sleep scores; a full personal baseline and a functional test protocol are still to be established.</p>
  <h2 id="score-milestones">Two score milestones</h2>
  <div class="tablewrap"><table aria-label="Proposed Oura score milestones"><thead><tr><th scope="col">Milestone</th><th scope="col">Success criterion</th><th scope="col">Current status</th></tr></thead><tbody>
    <tr><th scope="row">14-day average Oura score ≥85</th><td>14-calendar-day average Oura Sleep Score ≥85; at least 12 valid recorded nights</td><td>Proposed. {ouraEntries.length} valid Oura {ouraEntries.length === 1 ? "score" : "scores"} available; assessment pending.</td></tr>
    <tr><th scope="row">Higher sustained grading</th><td>28-calendar-day average ≥90; ≥80% of observed nights ≥85; at least 24 valid nights</td><td>Stretch proposal. Feasibility is unknown.</td></tr>
  </tbody></table></div>
  <p>Oura defines 85–100 as “Optimal.” The 14/28-day windows, coverage rules and 90-point stretch are deliberate choices for this project, not validated biological thresholds. A single 95-point night does not satisfy either sustained milestone. <a href="https://support.ouraring.com/hc/en-us/articles/360025445574-Sleep-Score">Oura definitions</a>.</p>
  <p>{latestOura ? <>The latest eligible whole-night Oura score is <strong>{latestOura.oura.score}</strong> on <a href={`/sleep/record/#night-${latestOura.id}`}>{latestOura.wake_date}</a>.</> : "No Oura sleep score is available yet."} {latestCoros ? <>The latest available COROS score is <strong>{latestCoros.coros.sleep_score}</strong> on <a href={`/sleep/record/#night-${latestCoros.id}`}>{latestCoros.wake_date}</a>.</> : "No COROS sleep score is available yet."} Each remains a separate benchmark. We do not switch providers to select the higher score or average their grades.</p>
  {latest && (!latest.oura || !latest.coros) && <p>The latest diary is <a href={`/sleep/record/#night-${latest.id}`}>{latest.wake_date}</a>. {!latest.oura && !latest.coros ? "Both providers' estimates are pending" : !latest.oura ? "Oura's estimate is pending" : "COROS's estimate is pending"}; the available earlier grades have not been assigned to it or counted as new scores.</p>}
  {latest?.oura?.score_eligible === false && <p>The latest date, <a href={`/sleep/record/#night-${latest.id}`}>{latest.wake_date}</a>, has an incomplete Oura recording. Its returned {latest.oura.score} is preserved with a coverage warning and excluded from whole-night milestones. The preceding eligible score has not been assigned to this date; COROS remains a separate series.</p>}
  <p><strong>Current practical question, 22 October:</strong> restore full recording coverage and compare an earlier bedtime with experienced sleep quality. The <a href="/sleep/record/#night-2026-10-22">latest diary</a> describes middling sleep, bed around 00:40 and uncertain waking after 10:00, perhaps 10:15. COROS returned 91 and 9h15m asleep in a 00:44–10:07 episode. Oura stopped at 06:30 after reported low battery; its 73 and timing-100 grade are partial-recording outputs, not full-night results or achievements. Ten dated entries contain nine eligible Oura whole-night scores. The user wants an earlier bedtime without specifying a new exact target; no sustained milestone is awarded.</p>

  <p><strong>21 October comparison:</strong> can fewer remembered awakenings repeat alongside sufficient sleep and useful daytime function? Today’s <a href="/sleep/record/#night-2026-10-21">record</a> adds fewer—but still several—awakenings, a roughly 00:10 bedtime and 10:15 waking, with initial sleepiness clearing within 10–15 minutes. Oura returned 90 and COROS 91, estimating 9h09m30s and 9h33m asleep. Oura restfulness fell 88 → 85 and timing 66 → 56; the lower grade does not erase the subjective improvement. Last food around 19:00–20:00 was approximately 4h10m–5h10m before bed; other routine changes remain unknown. Nine recorded dates do not complete either sustained milestone. Thresholds and inclusion rules remain unchanged.</p>

  <p><strong>20 October comparison:</strong> can rising device grades coexist with more comfortable sleep and useful daytime function? Oura returned <strong>92</strong>, its highest recorded grade; COROS returned <strong>89</strong>. Estimated sleep was <strong>8h55m30s and 9h04m</strong>, respectively. Oura timing rose <strong>51 → 66</strong>, still its lowest contributor, while restfulness stayed <strong>88</strong>. The diary describes <strong>okay-ish sleep and many brief awakenings</strong>, plus plentiful dream recall. Last food around 18:30 preceded a roughly midnight bedtime; small water sips were unquantified. The <a href="/sleep/record/#night-2026-10-20">record</a> retains that experience and the different device waking estimates. The follow-up reports waking around 09:30 feeling super sleepy, trying unsuccessfully to sleep more for 5–10 minutes, then deciding to get up. No awakening led to a bathroom trip; the brief episodes were estimated under 30 seconds, with dreams suspected as triggers. The second follow-up clarifies normal functioning within 10–15 minutes, with sustained later-day experience unreported. The user finds COROS closer to awake-period timing and Oura probably closer to total awake duration; these impressions remain separate from independent validation. The recent Oura sequence 90, 91, 92 is progress on the score benchmark; eight dates do not meet the assessment window. Continue comparing timing, comfortable sleep and function through the <a href="/sleep/research/personal-experiments/#the-current-routine-proposal">routine history</a>. No sustained milestone is awarded; thresholds and inclusion rules are unchanged.</p>
  <h3>How a milestone is assessed</h3>
  <p>Choose the calendar window before assessing it; count each provider wake date once. Show every date, the number of valid scores and missing dates. Include recorded travel, illness and difficult nights in the headline result, with contextual subgroup analyses labeled separately. No imputation of missing scores. Known incomplete recordings, including a battery interruption, are flagged and excluded from whole-night milestone calculations regardless of whether their grades are high or low. Preserve their returned values and their calendar dates as coverage gaps; do not substitute another provider’s score. The October 22 record is the first such flagged case. If an algorithm changes, mark that boundary before interpreting a trend. Retain the component distributions, not just the mean.</p>
  <p>Coverage alone does not fix selective missingness. If low-quality or difficult nights are disproportionately absent, the window needs review before a milestone is awarded. Two well-measured windows can test whether a result is repeatable.</p>
  <h2 id="physiology-and-function">Targets beyond a composite score</h2>
  <div class="tablewrap"><table aria-label="Directions for sleep optimization"><thead><tr><th scope="col">Dimension</th><th scope="col">Direction</th><th scope="col">What is justified now</th></tr></thead><tbody>
    <tr><th scope="row"><a href="/sleep/measurements/duration/">Duration</a></th><td>Appropriate range</td><td>Regularly obtain enough sleep for functioning. Adult consensus supports ≥7h; 7–9h is a starting reference range, not a personal optimum or strict upper bound.</td></tr>
    <tr><th scope="row"><a href="/sleep/measurements/timing/">Regularity and alignment</a></th><td>Investigate and improve</td><td>Measure variation and phase context. No 08:00 wake-time target or universal minute limit has been adopted.</td></tr>
    <tr><th scope="row"><a href="/sleep/measurements/continuity/">Continuity</a></th><td>Reduce meaningful disruption</td><td>Interpret latency and waking alongside duration and experience. Do not improve efficiency by simply cutting sleep opportunity.</td></tr>
    <tr><th scope="row"><a href="/sleep/measurements/stages/">REM and deep sleep</a></th><td>Monitor; test hypotheses</td><td>No independently justified personal minute quota. Verify measurement before making a small stage change the objective.</td></tr>
    <tr><th scope="row"><a href="/sleep/measurements/cardiac/">HR and HRV</a></th><td>Contextual trends</td><td>No universal minimum HR or maximum HRV. Compare the same metric within a device and matched intervals between devices.</td></tr>
    <tr><th scope="row"><a href="/sleep/measurements/breathing/">Breathing and oxygen</a></th><td>Monitoring and question-led assessment</td><td>Provider summaries do not establish absence or severity of a breathing disorder. A mean rate is not a full respiratory pattern.</td></tr>
    <tr><th scope="row"><a href="/sleep/measurements/temperature/">Temperature</a></th><td>Interpret the specific signal</td><td>Peripheral deviation, room temperature and core temperature are separate measurements. No “lowest possible” goal.</td></tr>
    <tr><th scope="row"><a href="/sleep/measurements/functioning/">Waking performance</a></th><td>Direct improvement</td><td>Select a validated vigilance protocol; establish personal variability before choosing a numerical improvement target.</td></tr>
  </tbody></table></div>
  <p>The duration reference comes from adult consensus and the National Sleep Foundation's age-specific recommendations. Their purpose is population guidance. An individual optimum requires repeated observations and suitable outcomes. <a href="https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4442216/">AASM/SRS statement</a>; <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/29073412/">NSF recommendations</a>.</p>
  <h2 id="functional-check">A functional check alongside the score</h2>
  <p>Start with a validated psychomotor vigilance task rather than treating a generic reaction-time game as equivalent. Keep its duration, thresholds, device and testing conditions consistent. Retain lapses, false starts and reaction-time distribution, plus time since waking and caffeine exposure. Practice and hardware latency can move results independently of sleep.</p>
  <p>The future target is a repeatable improvement beyond baseline variability, with no increase in false starts or unacceptable cost in sleep time, training or daily functioning. Its numerical size remains <strong>unresolved</strong> until a protocol and baseline exist. A rested baseline may already have very few lapses; a suitable response-time distribution or another validated outcome may then offer a more informative challenge. <a href="/sleep/measurements/functioning/">Measurement choices</a>.</p>
  <h2 id="environmental-references">A measurable environmental reference: light</h2>
  <p>Expert consensus for healthy adults aged 18–55 with regular daytime schedules offers quantitative light-exposure references using <strong>melanopic equivalent daylight illuminance (melanopic EDI)</strong>, measured at the eye:</p>
  <div class="tablewrap"><table aria-label="Expert-consensus light-exposure references"><thead><tr><th scope="col">Period</th><th scope="col">Reference</th><th scope="col">Current status</th></tr></thead><tbody>
    <tr><th scope="row">Daytime</th><td>At least 250 lux melanopic EDI</td><td>Exposure not measured</td></tr>
    <tr><th scope="row">Three hours before habitual sleep</th><td>Less than 10 lux melanopic EDI</td><td>Exposure not measured</td></tr>
    <tr><th scope="row">During sleep</th><td>Less than 1 lux melanopic EDI</td><td>Exposure not measured</td></tr>
  </tbody></table></div>
  <p>These are exposure recommendations derived from expert review, not a randomized demonstration of my personal optimum. Ordinary photopic lux or an uncalibrated phone reading cannot substitute for melanopic EDI. Spectrum, direction and measurement position matter. The paper discloses lighting-industry relationships among some authors. <a href="https://journals.plos.org/plosbiology/article?id=10.1371/journal.pbio.3001571">Brown and colleagues, 2022</a>.</p>
  <p>The useful next question is whether the current light pattern conflicts with sleep and waking goals, and whether a feasible change improves a defined outcome. That is more informative than buying equipment solely to fill a table. <a href="/sleep/research/improvement-strategies/#light-is-a-physiological-input">Light investigation</a>.</p>
  <h2 id="progression">How the project expands</h2>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Establish the measurement:</strong> enough repeated nights, units, source and limitations to know what the proposed target means.</li>
    <li><strong>Pursue a specific improvement:</strong> select a practical change and its expected effect before examining the result.</li>
    <li><strong>Check repetition and function:</strong> retain successful and unsuccessful nights, functional observations and confounding context.</li>
    <li><strong>Expand:</strong> add an outcome, a reference measurement or a new hypothesis when it could change a consequential decision.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>Expansion can begin before a score milestone is achieved. A clinically relevant issue or a promising measurement question should not wait for 90 points. Additional instrumentation earns its place by answering a question; the number of measured variables is not itself the achievement.</p>
  <h2 id="what-success-establishes">What a result would establish</h2>
  <p>Three decisions remain separate: <strong>was the benchmark achieved, should the intervention be retained, and what broader benefit was demonstrated?</strong> A change can achieve the score criterion while worsening functioning or imposing an unacceptable burden. The grade is still recorded, but that change need not be retained as an overall improvement.</p>
  <p>A score milestone establishes consistently meeting a named vendor's grading criteria. A reproducible task improvement establishes improvement on that task under the recorded conditions. A controlled personal comparison provides evidence about a change in my setting. None of these alone establishes comprehensive health, better memory in every domain, or added years of life.</p>
  <p>The goal remains ambitious. Its claims become stronger as the measurements, comparisons and outcomes improve. <a href="/sleep/research/">Investigations</a> hold the reasoning that can revise these targets.</p>
  <h2 id="target-history">Target history</h2>
  <p><strong>13 October 2026:</strong> initial specification. An 85-average benchmark follows Oura's grading category; a 90-average stretch is a chosen challenge with feasibility still unknown. Specific wake time and stage quotas remain unassigned pending investigation. No milestone has been achieved or awarded.</p>
  <p><strong>14 October 2026:</strong> clarified that the first milestone is an average ≥85, not a requirement that every night receive Optimal grading. Thresholds, windows and coverage rules are unchanged. Added the current thermal-comfort/continuity question; its routine check remains a proposal.</p>
  <p><strong>15 October 2026:</strong> a comfortable but shorter main sleep shifts the immediate question toward sufficient opportunity and final waking. Both providers reported REM despite no recalled dreams. Score milestones are unchanged; midnight and earlier meal/drink timings are proposals, with their benefit unresolved.</p>
  <p><strong>16 October 2026:</strong> the diary was initially recorded with both providers pending. Oura became available after device/app synchronization and returned a 90-point score and an estimated 8h 37m 30s asleep; COROS remained unavailable at that check, then a later on-demand retrieval returned 98 and 8h59 asleep. Both providers are now recorded with their separate retrieval times. The account reports more opportunity, continuity until a morning water break, and a positive impression. Sleepiness later on 15 October is retained alongside its alert first impression. Earlier meal timing, sleep timing, activity and light changed together, so this is not an isolated meal-timing result. No sustained milestone has been awarded; score thresholds and coverage rules are unchanged.</p>
  <p><strong>17 October 2026:</strong> recorded the social-night exception with its shorter sleep, late food/alcohol, uncomfortable floor and lower HRV. The date stays in all applicable headline windows. Added the retrospective refreshed-day report for October 16 and the separate October 17 follow-up: awake and sharp on waking, working and feeling good so far, with the rest of the day unassessed. Returning to the earlier routine is planned, not yet an observed recovery; no milestone has been awarded or redefined.</p>
  <p><strong>18 October 2026:</strong> added a longer night with scores 90/98 and Oura total-sleep/deep-sleep contributors 100. High grading occurred with a roughly four-hour food interval and two-hour gym-to-bed interval, broadening the comparison beyond the earlier six-hour meal gap. Timing 46 remains the weakest contributor. The follow-up reports feeling awake on waking October 18 and feeling good during October 17; October 18 later-day experience remains unreported. This records another high-scoring night, not completion of a 14- or 28-day milestone. The October 17 exception remains included; no threshold or coverage rule changed.</p>
  <p><strong>19 October 2026:</strong> added matching 91-point grades with close sleep totals, while preserving different episode windows and waking estimates. The diary explicitly reports no urinary awakening after 500 mL water at 22:30–23:00. The follow-up adds approximate 00:10–10:30 opportunity, onset taking a while and initial sleepiness resolving to the usual pace within 10–15 minutes; out-of-bed time and later-day experience remain unreported. Marked Oura firmware 2.1.36 at retrieval after previously observing 2.1.25; update timing and effect are unknown. Seven recorded dates do not complete either sustained milestone. Thresholds and inclusion rules remain unchanged.</p>
  <p><strong>20 October 2026:</strong> added Oura 92/COROS 89 and the okay-ish impression, many brief awakenings and plentiful dreams. Oura timing rose 51 → 66; its composite improved while estimated sleep shortened and restfulness stayed 88. The recent 90, 91, 92 series does not complete either sustained milestone. The follow-up adds waking around 09:30, strong sleepiness, an unsuccessful 5–10-minute attempt to sleep more, no bathroom trips after awakenings and estimated under-30-second wake episodes. Dreams remain a suspected trigger. The second follow-up clarifies feeling normal within 10–15 minutes, vivid dream recall, no awareness of breathing symptoms, and different subjective strengths for device awake-period timing versus total duration. Sustained later-day experience remains unreported; native estimates are unchanged. No previous-night water amount or other unreported practice was carried forward. Firmware 2.1.36 and algorithm v2 match the preceding retrieval. Thresholds and inclusion rules are unchanged.</p>
  <p><strong>21 October 2026:</strong> added the fewer-awakenings report, approximate diary timing, short-lived morning sleepiness and 19:00–20:00 last food. Both providers estimated more sleep and less awake time, while Oura’s composite/restfulness fell and COROS’s composite rose. Unusable Oura saturation is unavailable. No new cutoff, sustained milestone or causal conclusion is adopted; previous entries and inclusion rules remain unchanged.</p>
  <p><strong>22 October 2026:</strong> added a middling diary and COROS 91 alongside the Oura recording ending at 06:30 after reported low battery. Preserved Oura 73 and all component values with an incomplete flag. Explicitly excluded known incomplete recordings from whole-night milestones and high-component achievements without changing numeric thresholds, windows or coverage requirements. Latest eligible Oura score remains October21’s 90; October22 remains visible as a coverage gap. Earlier bedtime is the reported direction, not an established optimum.</p>
</Post>
